{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 41, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals dusting the stone paths like nature’s confetti. No itinerary, no rush. Just wandering through Gion’s quiet alleys, where wooden machiya houses whispered centuries of tea ceremonies and geisha whispers.\n\nAt Nishiki Market, I ate grilled mochi drizzled with sweet soy, fresh sashimi so bright it looked like stained glass, and matcha ice cream that tasted like spring itself. An elderly vendor smiled, handed me a free sample, and said, “Oishii?” I nodded, too full to speak.\n\nIn Tokyo, the city pulsed like a living circuit board. Shibuya’s scramble crossing was chaos turned poetry. I lost myself in Akihabara’s neon glow, then found peace in Meiji Shrine’s serene forest—a sudden hush after the subway’s roar.\n\nOne morning, I took the train to Nikko. The wooden bridges, golden shrines, and moss-covered statues felt like stepping into a painting. At a small ryokan that night, I slept on tatami mats, bathed in an onsen under a starlit sky, and ate kaiseki: tiny, perfect dishes that told stories of season and soil.\n\nThe Japanese don’t say much, but they show everything. A bow. A warm towel. A quiet gesture of offering tea. I didn’t need translation.\n\nOn my last day, I bought a single origami crane from a girl in Asakusa. She folded it slowly, eyes focused, then placed it in my palm with a nod. I didn’t ask why. I knew.\n\nJapan doesn’t demand your attention—it earns it. In silence, in scent, in the way a teacup is set just so. I came for the sights. I left with stillness.\n\nI’m already planning my return. Not to see more—but to feel more.", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.45900171995162964}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.26518285274505615}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.12355493754148483}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.38692519068